-- title:
--   Hopf–Galois criterion via the kernel of π
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring, let $A$ be a commutative ring carrying a Hopf $R$-algebra structure and flat as an $R$-module, let $B$ be a commutative ring carrying an $R$-bialgebra structure, and let $\pi \colon A \to B$ be a homomorphism of $R$-bialgebras which is surjective as a map of sets. Write $\rho = (\mathrm{id}_A \otimes \pi) \circ \Delta_A \colon A \to A \otimes_R B$ for the algebra map [`HopfAlgebra.coaction`](def/HopfAlgebra_HopfKer.html#L13) $\pi$, and let [`HopfAlgebra.hopfKer`](def/HopfAlgebra_HopfKer.html#L19) $\pi$ be the $R$-subalgebra of $A$ on which $\rho$ agrees with $a \mapsto a \otimes 1$. The assertion is an equivalence. The left-hand side, [`HopfAlgebra.IsHopfGalois`](def/HopfAlgebra_HopfKer.html#L66) $\pi$, says that the $R$-linear map [`HopfAlgebra.canMap`](def/HopfAlgebra_HopfKer.html#L34) $\pi \colon A \otimes_R A \to A \otimes_R B$ underlying the algebra homomorphism [`HopfAlgebra.canAlgHom`](def/HopfAlgebra_HopfKer.html#L26) $\pi$ is surjective, and that every element of its kernel lies in the $R$-submodule spanned by the balancing relations $(a h) \otimes a' - a \otimes (h a')$ with $a, a' \in A$ and $h \in$ [`HopfAlgebra.hopfKer`](def/HopfAlgebra_HopfKer.html#L19) $\pi$. The right-hand side says that the kernel of $\pi$, viewed as a ring homomorphism, is contained in the ideal of $A$ generated by the set of those $a \in$ [`HopfAlgebra.hopfKer`](def/HopfAlgebra_HopfKer.html#L19) $\pi$ with $\varepsilon_A(a) = 0$.
--
--   This is the Takeuchi–Schneider criterion for a faithfully flat Hopf–Galois extension (an $N$-torsor structure on $G \to \operatorname{Spec} A^{\mathrm{co}\pi}$, where $N = \operatorname{Spec} B$ sits inside $G = \operatorname{Spec} A$): the canonical map is bijective up to the balancing relations exactly when the Hopf ideal $\ker \pi$ is generated by the augmentation ideal of the coinvariant subalgebra. It is the criterion used by the project's further Hopf–Galois results, including the cocommutative finite-type case, reduction to local base rings by flat descent, and the statement producing faithful flatness and finite type of [`HopfAlgebra.hopfKer`](def/HopfAlgebra_HopfKer.html#L19) $\pi$.
